This bundle fronts the orchestrator and Vortex with `agentic-proxy`, which serves a certificate for
`${tls_server_name}` on port 8443, and on 8444 for Vortex. The generator issued it, and the one for
`sonarqube-tls-proxy`, from a CA of its own: `tls/ca.crt` in this directory. Your SonarQube JVM
must trust that CA, or every call to the orchestrator and Vortex fails the TLS handshake:

```
cp "$JAVA_HOME/lib/security/cacerts" /opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks
chmod u+w /opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks
keytool -importcert -noprompt -alias agentic-bundle-ca -file <this bundle>/tls/ca.crt \
  -keystore /opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks -storepass changeit
```

Starting from the JDK's `cacerts` keeps the public CAs SonarQube needs for other outbound calls.
Then in `sonar.properties` (append to any options you already set there), and restart SonarQube:

```
sonar.web.javaAdditionalOpts=-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore=/opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks -Djavax.net.ssl.trustStorePassword=changeit
sonar.ce.javaAdditionalOpts=-Djavax.net.ssl.trustStore=/opt/sonarqube-truststore.jks -Djavax.net.ssl.trustStorePassword=changeit
```

Regenerating this bundle into the same directory keeps `tls/` and the certificates, so you import
the CA once. The certificates are valid for 825 days. To issue new ones, for example after changing
`--tls-server-name` or `--sonarqube-external-host`, delete `tls/` and regenerate, then import the new
`tls/ca.crt` here and copy the new `sonarqube-tls-proxy/tls/` to your SonarQube machine.

To serve your own certificate from `agentic-proxy` instead, replace `tls/agentic-proxy/tls.crt` (the
full chain) and `tls/agentic-proxy/tls.key`, import the CA that issued it here instead, and run
`docker compose up -d --force-recreate agentic-proxy`.
